/// Calling a built-in with the wrong number of arguments raises, catchably.
///
/// Same rule as ArityFunction.a24 and the same sentence, which ALGOL-24.md
/// lists under Run-time errors: 'Expected 3 arguments but got 1.'  A built-in
/// is a function, and nothing in the specification gives it a different rule.
///
/// ⚠️ Kept in its own file, and not folded into ArityFunction.a24, because the
/// compiled half fails at EMIT time and takes the whole translation unit with
/// it.  Sharing a file, this defect would stop the other one's tests from ever
/// being emitted, and the harness would report one failure where there are two.
///
/// ⚠️ The compiled half refuses with 'A call to ''Copy'' is not supported by
/// the C back end yet.' -- which is false, and that is the interesting part
/// rather than a detail of wording.  Copy IS supported; CEmitter.Builtin keys
/// its table on name AND arity ('Copy/3'), so a call with the wrong count
/// misses the entry and falls through to the Unsupported(...) path meant for
/// constructs the back end has not implemented.  A programmer reading it goes
/// looking for a missing feature instead of a typo in their own call.
///
/// So there are two faults here, and a fix wants both: the program is refused
/// where the specification says it should run and raise, and the sentence
/// blames the wrong thing.
///
/// Interpreted: the test passes.
/// Compiled:    no C is emitted at all, so nothing runs.
///
test 'A Built-In With Too Few Arguments Raises';
begin
    var Caught := '';

    try
        Copy ('hello');
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Expected 3 arguments but got 1.', Caught);
end
